#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37209e5f-a6e8-55d5-b76f-19f79db67566" :knowledge-point/title "Computing 4x4 Determinants Using Row and Column Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "85acf106-2f03-5509-8ad0-08b82ef2a4c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8005" :question/problem "Calculate the value of\n$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of $a_{31}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of $a_{31}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\mid C_{2}:=C_{2} + 2C_{1}; C_{3}:=C_{3} + 3C_{1}; C_{4}:=C_{4} + (-3)C_{1}; =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot (-1)^{3 + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 4 \\cdot (-1)^{2 + 2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 4(11 - 3); = 32\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119614 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b0edd60-2801-59d3-b9df-18bfbf8763bb" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "4a9954b2-3d4b-5669-88cd-6e74c9683a60" :question/math-academy-id "e-8054" :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{11}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries below $a_{11}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1}; R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-4)R_{1}; R_{4}:=R_{4} + 3R_{1}; =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st column, we get\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot (-1)^{1 + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}; =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ff3b37-83e6-506a-a6e8-07858a2bcb66" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Column Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "ccf558b9-bf00-58c7-b244-2c5797f93d8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8060" :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\mid C_{1}:=C_{1} + C_{3}; C_{2}:=C_{2} + (-2)C_{3}; C_{4}:=C_{4} + 3C_{3}; =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 & 11\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot (-1)^{2 + 3}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = -\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 11\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ff3b37-83e6-506a-a6e8-07858a2bcb66" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8093b1f1-d3ed-5ed8-a9e5-08dbda817a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119307} {:answer/id #uuid "8a478fe5-40a9-51bb-abfb-dceb527a4108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -7\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119308} {:answer/id #uuid "aea34567-748e-5267-ad16-f14eea2221b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119309} {:answer/id #uuid "d700cc92-46e0-58d9-b1e9-9f6fa1a9f666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -6